Let me analyze the race. In `flight.js`, `run`:

```js
const promise = Promise.resolve().then(work).then(value => {
  pending.delete(key);
  commit(value);
  return value;
});
pending.set(key, promise);
```

The bug: when the flight settles, it does `pending.delete(key)` unconditionally. If the flight was detached (via `forget` — invalidate/set/clear), and a *new* flight for the same key was started, then when the old flight settles it deletes the *new* flight from pending and commits its stale value into `values`. Two bugs:

1. `pending.delete(key)` removes the newer flight from the registry, breaking dedup for readers arriving between the old flight's settle and the new flight's settle — new readers would start duplicate loads (loses dedup).
2. `commit(value)` stores the stale value unconditionally — after `invalidate`, the stale value overwrites/gets cached. That's the "serves an old value after a key was invalidated" bug.

Also, after `set(key, value)`, an old detached load's completion commits its value over the set value and sets a new deadline ("their later completion must not restart its TTL") — bug as well.

Also, `forget`/`clear` detached flights: on settle, `pending.delete(key)` could delete a newer flight — same issue.

Fix: on settle, only delete if `pending.get(key) === promise` (self-identity check), and only commit if still the registered flight. But the promise references itself — need to construct carefully:

```js
run(key, work, commit) {
  if (pending.has(key)) return pending.get(key);
  let promise;
  promise = Promise.resolve().then(work).then(value => {
    if (pending.get(key) === promise) {
      pending.delete(key);
      commit(value);
    }
    return value;
  });
  pending.set(key, promise);
  return promise;
}
```

Wait, but there's a subtlety with rejection: the commit callback only runs on fulfillment. On rejection, `pending.delete(key)` never runs — rejected loads must not be cached (fine) but the pending entry stays forever! Contract: "Rejected loads ... are never cached: all waiters receive the same rejection reason, and a subsequent get may retry." A subsequent `get` would call `run`, see `pending.has(key)` and return the already-rejected promise — so subsequent gets return the same rejection forever, never retrying. That's a bug: rejection leaves the flight in `pending` forever.

Actually wait — since `pending.get(key)` returns the rejected promise, all subsequent gets return the same rejection. The contract says "a subsequent get may retry" — may, so it's allowed either way? "may retry" — permissive. But also the waiters: "all waiters receive the same rejection reason". Hmm, but leaving rejected promise in pending means it stays forever as "active flight" — but `size` doesn't count flights, fine. Actually the phrase "a subsequent get may retry" permits not retrying. But there's another issue: unhandled rejection. Actually the original readers get the rejection. Hmm, but leaving it in the map means the flight is never detached. Contract says "Missing keys load independently; concurrent reads of the same key share one active load." A rejected promise is no longer active. "ttlMs = 0 still deduplicates an unsettled flight but never reuses a settled value" — settled includes rejected presumably. Hmm.

The safer fix: on rejection, also remove from pending (with identity check) so subsequent gets retry. That satisfies "may retry".

